  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B015",
        "laureates": [
          "Christopher Jarzynski",
          "Denis J. Evans",
          "Gavin E. Crooks"
        ],
        "citation": "For exact relations connecting nonequilibrium work, dissipation, and fluctuations."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B008",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The fluctuation and work relations expose a simple, quantitative structure beneath irreversible processes: they connect measurable work distributions to equilibrium free-energy differences and constrain entropy production away from equilibrium. Their reach across statistical mechanics, molecular-scale experiments, and modern nonequilibrium theory makes them a foundational achievement. The three credited contributions are related but identifiable: Evans and colleagues established fluctuation theorems, Jarzynski derived the nonequilibrium work relation, and Crooks connected forward and reverse processes.",
    "case_against": "The ballot bundles closely related results rather than a single sharply bounded discovery, and their broad influence may be less visible outside statistical physics than that of some experimental advances. The committee should verify that the three contributions form a coherent prize citation and that later generalizations are not being mistaken for the original work.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth ranked B015 eighth and noted that, while the relations are established and tested, their lasting impact across physics is less settled than their importance within nonequilibrium statistical mechanics.",
        "response": "That is the central reservation, but I would give the results substantial weight precisely because they turn nonequilibrium behavior into exact, testable statements rather than a collection of system-specific descriptions. Their importance is not limited to one application: the relations link statistical mechanics to experiments on driven small systems and provide a framework for interpreting fluctuations and dissipation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson viewed optical lattice clocks as mature instruments, while questioning whether the prize case rests on a transformative measurement capability or on specific completed discoveries enabled by it.",
        "response": "That distinction is useful for assessing B008, which I keep as an alternative: a mature and powerful platform can merit recognition, but the citation should identify its foundational advance rather than rely on future tests. For B015 the central results themselves are completed theoretical discoveries with experimental tests, so the case is less dependent on what later applications may achieve."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The precise boundary among the original fluctuation theorem, Jarzynski relation, Crooks relation, and subsequent generalizations needs careful historical review.",
      "The scale of the relations' impact beyond statistical physics should be assessed against other shortlisted achievements."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile; no direct professional connection to the proposed laureates is identified."
  }
}
